3.2 Moves

he previous section ended with a promise: every value in a Rust program has exactly one owner, and when that owner goes out of scope the value is dropped. This section looks at what that rule does to code you already know how to write. Consider the following listing.

Listing 3.4: Assigning a String to a second variable
fn main() {
let a = String::from("ferris");
let b = a;
println!("{}", b);
}

The String type is a small struct on the stack: a pointer to the bytes on the heap, a length and a capacity. On a 64-bit target these three fields take 24 bytes. When we write let b = a, the compiler copies those 24 bytes into b, but it does not copy the heap bytes. If both a and b were still valid, both would try to free the same heap allocation when they went out of scope, a bug known as a double free. Rust prevents it by declaring that a has been moved into b.

pages.52:
After a move, the original variable can no longer be used. The compiler enforces this at compile time, so there is no run-time cost and no run-time check. Listing 3.5 tries to use a after the move.

Listing 3.5: Using a moved value
fn main() {
let a = String::from("ferris");
let b = a;
println!("{} {}", a, b);
}

This program does not compile. The compiler reports error E0382, borrow of moved value: a, and points at the line where the value moved. Notice that the error is about the use of a, not about the assignment itself: the assignment on its own is perfectly legal.

Moves are not limited to assignments. Passing a value to a function moves it into the function's parameter, and returning a value moves it out to the caller. Listing 3.6 shows a function that takes ownership and gives it back.

Listing 3.6: Taking and returning ownership
fn shout(s: String) -> String {
let mut t = s;
t.push_str("!");
t
}
fn main() {
let name = String::from("ferris");
let name = shout(name);
println!("{}", name);
}

pages.53:
The second let name in Listing 3.6 shadows the first. This is allowed, and it is what makes the pattern comfortable: the old name was moved into shout, so nothing can use it by mistake, and the new name takes its place.

Giving ownership to every function and getting it back in the return value works, but it quickly becomes tedious. Chapter 4 introduces references, which let a function use a value without owning it. For now the important point is that a move is the default behaviour for types that own heap memory: String, Vec<T>, Box<T> and the like.

When you really want two independent copies, you ask for one explicitly with the clone method. Listing 3.7 clones a before assigning.

Listing 3.7: Cloning instead of moving
fn main() {
let a = String::from("ferris");
let b = a.clone();
println!("{} {}", a, b);
}

This compiles, because b owns its own copy of the heap bytes. The price is an allocation and a copy of the text, which is why the language makes you write clone rather than doing it silently. The compiler's error message o ers this very suggestion ("consider cloning the value"), but the speci cation of what to clone, and whether you need a copy at all, is up to you.

pages.161:
7.1 Threads

he simplest way to run code concurrently is to start an operating system thread. The standard library does it with std::thread::spawn, which takes a closure and returns a JoinHandle. The new thread starts running the closure at once, while the thread that called spawn carries on with the next line. Nothing is shared between the two unless you arrange it.

Listing 7.1: A first thread
use std::thread;
fn main() {
let handle = thread::spawn(|| {
println!("hello from the new thread");
});
handle.join().unwrap();
}

The call to join blocks the calling thread until the spawned thread has finished, and it returns a Result: Ok with the value the closure returned, or Err if the thread panicked. If main returned without joining, the process would end and the spawned thread would be stopped wherever it happened to be, so the greeting might never be printed.

pages.162:
Most useful threads work on data that the parent owns. Listing 7.2 tries to print a vector from a thread.

Listing 7.2: Borrowing from a thread (does not compile)
use std::thread;
fn main() {
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let handle = thread::spawn(|| {
println!("{:?}", v);
});
handle.join().unwrap();
}

The compiler refuses, with error E0373: the closure may outlive the current function, but it borrows v. The compiler cannot know how long the new thread will run, so it cannot prove that v will still be there. The fix is the move keyword before the closure, which makes the closure take ownership of the variables it uses.

Listing 7.3: Moving the vector into the thread
use std::thread;
fn main() {
let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
println!("{:?}", v);
});
handle.join().unwrap();
}

After this change v belongs to the thread, and main can no longer use it. This is the same move we met in chapter 3, applied to a closure's captured variables. If main needs the data afterwards, the options are to clone it before the move or to share it through one of the types of section 7.3.

